\section{Introduction}
\todo{Make sure to avoid uneeded redundancy with the small intro before}
The previous chapter introduced \ac{PaTAS} and identified its first component, the Trust Assessment Module, whose role is to evaluate the trustworthiness of a training dataset and, by extension, of the input data presented to a \aclu{NN} at inference time. This chapter develops the methodological and formal content of that module.
The chapter is organised around three observations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Trustworthiness of a training dataset is not a scalar. Different trustworthiness properties (fairness, label correctness, representativeness, privacy compliance, robustness to leakage, etc.) call for different evidence, different evaluation granularities, and different aggregation rules. Treating them uniformly hides exactly the information that should drive downstream decisions.\todo{what does uniformly mean}
    \item Properties live at different levels of the data. Some properties are only meaningful at the dataset level (class balance, coverage, distribution gap with respect to a target population). Others are intrinsically per-entry (label correctness, annotator agreement on a specific item) or per-feature (sensor reliability, presence of sensitive attributes in a column). The same scheme used at inference time on individual inputs can be applied for entry- and feature-level dataset properties, which is what makes input-data trust assessment a special case of dataset trust assessment rather than a separate problem.
    \item Evidence about trustworthiness is rarely complete. Training datasets are typically constructed through multi-stage lifecycles in which documentation, metadata, and direct observations are partial, sometimes contradictory, and often unavailable for historical pipelines. The framework must therefore distinguish low support from no support — which is exactly what Subjective Logic offers and what scalar quality scores cannot.
\end{enumerate}
Building on these observations, this chapter presents a top-level framework for assessing the trustworthiness of AI training datasets. The framework is a direct specialisation of the General Trust Assessment Methodology of \cref{chap:Trust_method} to the dataset setting and provides the conceptual and formal substrate \todo{substrate?} for several specific contributions of this thesis: the assessment of sampling/representation bias~\cite{ecmldatatrust}, the assessment of labeling accuracy from annotator disagreement~\cite{trust-aidatatrust}, and the multi-stage analysis illustrated on COMPAS. These works are presented in \cref{sec:applications_data} as applications of the framework rather than as separate methodologies. The framework rests\todo{rests??} on four design principles introduced in 6.3.1 \cref{blabla}, which are then organised into a four-axis design space (§6.3.2) \cref{blabla} that we use throughout the remainder of the dissertation. Section 6.4\cref{blabla} gives the formal definitions and the three quantification schemes (\acs{BPQ}, \acs{EWQ}, \acs{CUQ}) used to map evidence into subjective opinions. Section 6.5 \cref{blabla} demonstrates the framework on three applications spanning different cells of the design space.






















\section{Sources of Uncertainty in Training Datasets}
Uncertainty is an inherent component of any assessment process, including but not limited to the evaluation of AI training datasets. More fundamentally, uncertainty does not arise from a single cause but from multiple, qualitatively different mechanisms. These mechanisms differ in whether they can be adequately modeled using classical probability theory or whether they require an evidence-based uncertainty framework such as Subjective Logic.

\subsection{Fundamental Sources of Uncertainty}

Across domains, uncertainty in trust assessment typically originates from the following sources:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Randomness in nature}, reflecting inherent variability in the variable (proposition to assess); \emph{e.g., repeated evaluations of the same process yielding different outcomes due to intrinsic variability, leading to uncertainty about whether the assessed system consistently satisfies a reliability property};
    
    \item \textbf{Noise in the evidence}, arising from measurement errors, annotation mistakes, or stochastic perturbations;
    \emph{e.g., imperfect or noisy observations providing fluctuating support for a trust proposition, such as inconsistent measurements or annotations affecting confidence in the assessment};
    \item \textbf{System complexity}, where interactions between components are only partially observable or poorly understood;
    \emph{e.g., uncertainty in trust arising from the interaction of multiple subsystems or lifecycle stages, where the contribution of individual components to the overall reliability cannot be cleanly isolated};


    \item \textbf{Conflicting evidence}, where different diagnostics or information sources support incompatible conclusions;
    \emph{e.g., one assessment indicating that a trust requirement is satisfied while another, based on a different perspective or metric, suggests the opposite};


    \item \textbf{Lack of evidence}, where relevant information is missing, incomplete, or unavailable.
    \emph{e.g., insufficient documentation or unavailable audit results preventing a confident determination of whether a trust property holds}.


\end{itemize}

While these sources often co-occur in practice, they differ significantly in how they should be modeled and interpreted. In the context of AI training datasets, each of these sources naturally arises at different stages of the data lifecycle.
These five sources are not abstract: they map directly onto stages and artifacts of the dataset lifecycle, which we use in §6.3 to organize the framework around lifecycle decomposition.

\subsection{Uncertainty Addressable by Classical Probability Theory}

Classical probability theory is well suited for modeling uncertainty that arises from inherent randomness and stochastic noise, commonly referred to as \emph{aleatoric uncertainty}. In this setting, uncertainty reflects variability in outcomes rather than a lack of knowledge.

In training datasets, such uncertainty may arise from stochastic measurement noise (e.g., sensor precision limits), natural variability in observed phenomena, or randomized sampling procedures applied to a well-defined population. For example, repeated measurements of a physical quantity may vary slightly due to sensor noise, or different images of the same object may exhibit natural variation in lighting and pose. When the data-generating process is well specified and sufficient observations are available, probability distributions can be estimated and used to characterize expected variability.

However, this probabilistic framework implicitly assumes that (i) the underlying process is known, (ii) observations are conditionally independent given the model, and (iii) the available evidence is neither contradictory nor structurally incomplete. These assumptions are frequently violated in real-world dataset construction.

\subsection{Uncertainty Requiring Evidence-Based Modeling}

The remaining sources of uncertainty (system complexity, conflicting evidence, and lack of evidence) cannot be adequately represented using classical probability theory alone. These sources give rise to \emph{epistemic uncertainty}, reflecting incomplete or unreliable knowledge about the dataset and its provenance.

\paragraph{System complexity.}
Complexity-induced uncertainty arises when a dataset results from multiple interacting processes, such as sampling strategies, collection protocols, annotation workflows, and preprocessing pipelines. For instance, a dataset may combine data collected across different time periods, devices, or geographic regions, each with distinct characteristics. Even if each individual step appears reasonable in isolation, their interaction may introduce unintended biases or distributional shifts that are difficult to model probabilistically.

\paragraph{Conflicting evidence.}
Conflicting evidence occurs when different trust sources provide incompatible signals about dataset reliability. For example, statistical metrics may indicate balanced group representation, while an audit of the data collection process reveals undocumented exclusion criteria affecting specific subpopulations. Similarly, high inter-annotator agreement may coexist with systematic label bias affecting only a minority group. In such cases, collapsing all signals into a single probability obscures the underlying disagreement.

\paragraph{Lack of evidence.}
Lack of evidence is common when key information about the dataset lifecycle is missing or inaccessible. Typical examples include absent documentation of preprocessing steps, incomplete metadata describing sampling decisions, or unavailable annotation guidelines. Importantly, the absence of evidence does not imply evidence of absence; rather, it introduces uncertainty that cannot be resolved through probabilistic estimation alone.

Classical probability theory lacks a principled mechanism to distinguish between low probability and unknown probability. As a result, missing or contradictory evidence is often implicitly collapsed into point estimates, leading to overconfident or misleading assessments. Evidence-based frameworks, and Subjective Logic in particular, address this limitation by explicitly representing belief, disbelief, and residual uncertainty as distinct quantities.

\subsection{Why All Sources of Uncertainty Occur in Training Datasets}

AI training datasets are especially prone to the coexistence of all aforementioned sources of uncertainty. Unlike controlled experimental data, training datasets are typically assembled through complex, multi-stage lifecycles involving heterogeneous actors, tools, and governance regimes.

Randomness and noise arise naturally from data collection and annotation processes. System complexity emerges from the interaction of sampling decisions, collection methods, annotation protocols, and data processing steps, often distributed across organizations or over time. Conflicting evidence frequently arises when datasets are evaluated using multiple fairness, bias, or quality metrics that emphasize different aspects of reliability. Lack of evidence is common due to missing metadata, undocumented preprocessing, proprietary data sources, or limited visibility into historical data collection practices.

Crucially, these uncertainties do not disappear simply because a dataset achieves high predictive performance or passes standard quality checks. On the contrary, dataset reliability is often assessed precisely in situations where evidence is partial, indirect, or contested. For this reason, training dataset assessment requires a formalism that can accommodate both probabilistic variability and epistemic limitations. Subjective Logic provides such a foundation by enabling uncertainty-aware reasoning that remains faithful to the available evidence without forcing unwarranted certainty.

Subjective Logic provides the representational machinery (belief, disbelief, vacuity), but on its own does not tell the analyst what propositions to formulate, which trust sources to consult, or how to combine them across the dataset lifecycle. Those choices are the role of the framework presented in the next section.


\section{Trust Assessment Framework for Training Datasets}
\label{sec:data_framework}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{img/data/framework.pdf}
    \caption{Overview of the proposed dataset trust assessment framework. Dataset trustworthiness is decomposed into lifecycle stages, including sampling, collection, annotation, and processing. Each stage is associated with trust sources providing evidence relevant to a given property (e.g., accuracy, bias, robustness). Evidence is aggregated under uncertainty using Subjective Logic.}
    \label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

This section presents a high-level overview of the proposed framework for assessing the trustworthiness of AI training datasets. The framework supports transparent and uncertainty-aware reasoning by integrating heterogeneous evidence across the dataset lifecycle. Rather than producing a single deterministic score, dataset trustworthiness is represented as a structured and property-dependent trust assessment that explicitly accounts for incomplete, conflicting, or missing information.

\subsection{Design Principles}

The proposed framework is built upon four key design principles.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Property-dependent assessment. Trustworthiness is always defined with respect to a specific property P of interest. The same dataset may be highly trustworthy for one property and not for another, in line with the trust-proposition formalism introduced in \cref{chap:Trust_method}.
    \item Lifecycle-based reasoning. Dataset trustworthiness emerges from the correctness of the processes that produce and transform the data. The framework therefore decomposes evaluation along the four stages of the dataset lifecycle: sampling, collection / measurement, annotation, and processing.
    \item Multi-granular evaluation. Some properties are meaningful only at the level of the dataset as a whole (e.g. class balance), others at the level of individual entries (e.g. label correctness), and others at the level of features (e.g. sensor reliability or presence of sensitive attributes). The framework supports trust sources operating at any of these three granularities and provides aggregation rules between them.
    \item Heterogeneous evidence integration under explicit uncertainty. The framework integrates statistical diagnostics, fairness metrics, privacy indicators, agreement measures, and documentation artifacts. Evidence is not collapsed into a point estimate; the residual uncertainty arising from conflict, missingness, or system complexity is preserved using Subjective Logic.
\end{enumerate}
\marginpar{Lifecycle-Based Decomposition}
Dataset trustworthiness is decomposed according to four main stages of the dataset lifecycle: sampling, collection, annotation, and processing. Each stage contributes differently to the overall reliability of the dataset.

Sampling determines the representativeness and coverage of the data, collection affects measurement validity and consent, annotation introduces labeling uncertainty and bias, and processing may introduce distributional distortions or data leakage. This decomposition enables targeted analysis and intervention, rather than treating dataset trustworthiness as a monolithic property.

\subsection{The Four-Axis Design Space}

A trust assessment of a training dataset is fully specified by four choices, which together form the design space of the framework:
\begin{table}[]
    \centering
    % \caption{Caption}
    \begin{tabular}{cccc}
    \textbf{Axis} & \textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Role}\\
    \hline
    Property & $P$ & accuracy, bias, representativeness, label correctness, privacy, … & what is being evaluated \\
    Lifecycle stage & S & sampling, collection, annotation, processing &  where in the lifecycle the evidence comes from \\
    Granularity & G & dataset, entry, feature & at what level the property is meaningful \\
    \end{tabular}
    \label{tab:placeholder}
\end{table}












\subsection{Trust Sources and Evidence Modeling}


Within each dataset lifecycle stage, the framework identifies trust sources that provide evidence relevant to dataset trustworthiness. These sources include statistical diagnostics, bias and fairness metrics, privacy risk indicators, annotation agreement measures, and documentation artifacts.

Importantly, trust assessment is inherently \emph{multi-granular}. Depending on the property under consideration, trust may be evaluated at different levels:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dataset-level properties}, such as global representativeness, class balance, or overall distribution alignment, which are assessed using aggregate statistics;
    
    \item \textbf{Entry-level properties}, such as label correctness, annotation consistency, or instance-level uncertainty, which are evaluated for individual data points;
    
    \item \textbf{Feature-level properties}, such as sensor reliability, measurement noise, or the presence of sensitive attributes, which are associated with specific input features.
\end{itemize}

This distinction is fundamental, as it determines how evidence is collected, structured, and interpreted. Dataset-level assessments provide global evidence, while entry-level and feature-level assessments enable fine-grained analysis of data reliability. The framework supports these different levels of granularity by allowing trust sources to operate at the appropriate level and by aggregating their contributions within a unified trust representation.

