Table~\ref{tab:cifar10h-results} summarizes the annotation-stage trust-source
metrics and the resulting BPQ subjective opinion for CIFAR-10H.

The CIFAR-10H dataset provides a contrasting case focused exclusively on
annotation reliability in a large-scale crowdsourcing context. We apply the
framework to the annotation stage using inter-annotator agreement, outlier label
rate, and annotator uncertainty scores. Table~2 summarizes the resulting BPQ
quantification.

The aggregated opinion for the annotation stage yields a belief of $b=0.652$, a
disbelief of $d=0.098$, and an uncertainty of $u=0.250$. Inter-annotator agreement
($q=0.792$) provides strong positive evidence, while low outlier label rates
($q=0.834$) and low annotator uncertainty ($q=0.982$) further support reliable
labeling. Despite these strong signals, uncertainty remains non-negligible,
reflecting inherent ambiguity in visual classification tasks and variability
across annotators.

This result illustrates a key distinction between agreement-based metrics and
trust-based assessment. While traditional metrics might suggest near-perfect
annotation quality, the subjective opinion makes explicit that high reliability
does not imply complete certainty.



